  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Cold front has moved well offshore late this \nafternoon and has ushered in strong CAA and ensuing gusty nrly \nwinds. There continues to be high confidence that a strong \ncoastal low turning nor'easter will form well off the Carolina \ncoast through the latter half of the weekend into early to mid \nnext week. Strong winds, high waves, and coastal flooding as \nwell as extremely dangerous marine conditions will be the \nresult. \n\nThere is still model uncertainty beyond early week on where the\nlow pressure area will be, but the trend in 17/12Z global\nguidance including ensemble packet has shifted a bit further\noffshore. Nevertheless, this does Not diminish any of the\nadvertised high coastal impacts, as long duration and long fetch\nnnerly winds will bring large waves and pile up water along the\ncoast, leading to coastal flooding well into the week. \n\nHigh winds on the coast still expected. Have fine-tuned the wind\nadv, taking W Carteret out but keeping the OBX and nern zones\ncloser to the stronger gradient winds in tact. High wind warning\ncriteria being met appear to be lessening, though can't rule out\na few gusts reaching 60 mph early this evening with the initial\nCAA surge. With the further offshore track during the week, this\nshould also keep winds lower.\n\n2-4 foot above ground level coastal flooding will be possible\nwith minor to moderate impacts throughout the Outer Banks, Core\nBanks and Downeast Carteret County. Impacts are expected to both\nsound and oceanside locales with potentially significant impacts\nto vulnerable areas on the ocean side. A mix of coastal flood\nheadlines is in effect to cover these hazards. Additionally, a \nhigh surf advisory is in effect for the Outer Banks with \nbreaking waves of generally 8-14 feet expected in the surf \nzone. A moderate to high risk of rip currents is also expected \nthrough much of the week.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...After record breaking heat the last several \ndays, temperatures are expected to cool significantly. Maximum \ntemperatures through the period are expected to remain in the \n60-70 degree range with cooler values inland and warmer \ntemperatures near the coast. Overnight lows will range from the \n40s inland just after the frontal passage to the 50s by the end\nof the week with coastal locations in the 50s early in the \nforecast period to near 60  by the end of next week. Winds will\nremain breezy, particularly in the afternoons depending upon \nthe eventual track of the offshore nor'easter. \n\nRegarding rain, main batch should end by this evening as area of\nfgen/lift shifts offshore. As the low meanders offshore, some\nwrap around rain may ocnl sneak back onshore, with best chances\non the OBX over the next several days.",
